/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · The 20th century and Australian history */
WB.L("great-depression", "history", "intermediate", "The Great Depression",
  "In 1929 the world economy began to collapse. By the early 1930s, a quarter or more of workers in countries like the US and Australia had no job, and the politics of the decade turned dark.",
  [
    ["The Roaring Twenties", "The 1920s boom in the US was fuelled partly by borrowing and stock speculation.",
      "After World War I, the United States boomed. Cars, radios, refrigerators and other electric appliances spread into ordinary homes, often bought on new instalment plans, and share prices soared. Many people bought shares 'on margin', paying a small deposit and borrowing the rest, betting prices would keep rising. That works while prices climb, but a modest fall can wipe out the buyer's whole stake. Farmers, meanwhile, struggled with falling prices after the wartime demand for food ended. The boom rested on shaky foundations: easy credit, unequal wealth, and a widespread belief that prices could only go up."],
    ["The crash", "The Wall Street Crash of October 1929 wiped out huge amounts of wealth.",
      "In late October 1929, panic selling hit the New York Stock Exchange. On 'Black Tuesday', 29 October, a record number of shares changed hands as prices collapsed, and brokers demanded that margin borrowers repay loans they could not cover. After a brief rally in 1930, the slide continued: by mid-1932 the market had lost about 89% of its peak value, and it did not regain that 1929 peak until 1954. People who had borrowed to buy shares were ruined. But the crash alone didn't cause the Depression; it was the start of a broader collapse in spending, lending and confidence."],
    ["Banks fail, jobs vanish", "Thousands of US banks failed, and unemployment reached about 25%.",
      "As frightened depositors rushed to withdraw savings, thousands of American banks failed, around 9,000 between 1930 and 1933, with no deposit insurance to protect savers. When a bank collapsed, families simply lost their money. Businesses cut production and workers, so people had less to spend, which led to more cuts: a vicious circle economists call a deflationary spiral. Prices fell, which made existing debts harder to repay. By 1933 about a quarter of American workers were unemployed. Shanty towns of the homeless, nicknamed 'Hoovervilles' after President Herbert Hoover, appeared on the edges of cities."],
    ["The Dust Bowl", "Drought and over-farming turned parts of the US Great Plains into a dust bowl.",
      "Nature added to the misery. In the 1920s, farmers on the southern Great Plains had ploughed up millions of hectares of native grassland to grow wheat. When severe drought struck in the early 1930s, the exposed topsoil dried and blew away in enormous dust storms. On 'Black Sunday', 14 April 1935, a wall of dust darkened skies across Oklahoma and Texas. Hundreds of thousands of people left the region, many heading west to California in search of farm work, a story told in John Steinbeck's novel The Grapes of Wrath. The disaster led to new soil conservation programs."],
    ["A global disaster", "The Depression spread worldwide; Australia's unemployment peaked near 30%.",
      "The collapse spread through trade and finance. American lenders called in loans made to Europe, and countries raised tariffs to protect their own industries, like the US Smoot-Hawley tariff of 1930. Others retaliated, and world trade shrank by around two-thirds in value between 1929 and 1934. Germany, already burdened by war reparations and foreign debt, was hit especially hard. Australia, dependent on exporting wool and wheat and on borrowing from London, suffered badly as export prices fell and credit dried up. Unemployment peaked at around 30% in 1932, among the highest in the developed world."],
    ["Australia in the Depression", "Australian governments argued fiercely over whether to cut spending or repay debts, and many families relied on 'the susso'.",
      "In 1930 Sir Otto Niemeyer of the Bank of England visited and urged Australian governments to cut spending to keep repaying their London loans. The 1931 Premiers' Plan did that, reducing wages, pensions and public spending. NSW Premier Jack Lang refused to pay interest to British bondholders, and in 1932 the NSW Governor, Sir Philip Game, dismissed him, a rare and still-debated event. Unemployed people received food relief known as 'the susso', short for sustenance, often in return for work. Many men went 'on the track', carrying a swag from town to town looking for jobs, and evictions sparked protests."],
    ["The response", "Governments experimented with new policies, including Roosevelt's New Deal.",
      "At first, many governments cut spending to balance budgets, which most economists now believe made things worse. In 1933, US President Franklin D. Roosevelt launched the New Deal: public works programs that built roads, dams and schools, bank reforms, federal deposit insurance and, in 1935, social security for older people. Britain's economist John Maynard Keynes argued, most famously in his 1936 General Theory, that governments should spend more during downturns to boost demand. Leaving the gold standard also helped countries recover by freeing them to lower interest rates. Recovery was slow, and full employment returned in many countries only with rearmament for World War II."],
    ["Lasting lessons", "The Depression fuelled extremism, and shaped modern economic policy.",
      "Mass unemployment and despair helped extremist movements, most catastrophically in Germany, where the Nazi Party's vote surged from under 3% in 1928 to about 37% in July 1932 as the economy collapsed. The Depression also shaped economics for generations. Deposit insurance, unemployment benefits, central banks acting as lenders of last resort, and government stimulus during recessions all grew from its lessons. They were used again in the 2008 global financial crisis, when Australia paid cash stimulus to households, and in the 2020 pandemic, with JobKeeper wage subsidies. The period is a reminder that economic hardship can reshape politics, not just household budgets."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was buying shares 'on margin'?", ["Buying cheap shares", "Paying a small deposit and borrowing the rest", "Buying from overseas", "Buying through a bank"], 1, "It magnified losses when prices fell."],
    [1, "What was Black Tuesday?", ["29 October 1929, a day of collapsing share prices", "A bank holiday", "The end of the Depression", "A war"], 0, "The market lost about 89% by 1932."],
    [2, "What were 'Hoovervilles'?", ["New suburbs", "Shanty towns of the homeless", "Government offices", "Factories"], 1, "Named after President Hoover."],
    [4, "Roughly how high did Australian unemployment go?", ["5%", "10%", "About 30%", "60%"], 2, "Among the highest in the developed world."],
    [6, "What was the New Deal?", ["A trade agreement", "Roosevelt's programs of public works and reforms", "A peace treaty", "A stock market rule"], 1, "Launched in 1933."],
  ]);

WB.L("holocaust", "history", "intermediate", "The Holocaust",
  "The systematic, state-sponsored murder of six million Jews by Nazi Germany. Understanding how it happened is essential to preventing it again.",
  [
    ["What it was", "The Holocaust was the Nazi regime's murder of about six million European Jews between 1941 and 1945.",
      "The Holocaust, also called the Shoah, meaning 'catastrophe' in Hebrew, was the deliberate, organised persecution and murder of European Jews by Nazi Germany and its collaborators. About six million Jews were killed, around two-thirds of Europe's Jewish population, including roughly 1.5 million children. Whole communities that had existed for centuries, in places like Poland, Lithuania and Hungary, were destroyed. The Nazis also murdered Roma and Sinti people, disabled people, Polish and Soviet civilians, Soviet prisoners of war, political opponents, gay men and others. Nazi ideology treated Jews as a 'race' to be eliminated entirely, which made their persecution uniquely total."],
    ["Step by step", "Persecution escalated gradually from discrimination to murder.",
      "The Holocaust didn't begin with killing. After Adolf Hitler came to power in 1933, Jewish businesses were boycotted and Jews were fired from government jobs, universities and the professions. The 1935 Nuremberg Laws stripped Jews of citizenship and banned marriage with non-Jews. In the Kristallnacht pogrom of November 1938, synagogues and Jewish shops were destroyed across Germany and around 30,000 Jewish men were sent to concentration camps. Many Jews tried to emigrate, but most countries, including Australia at the 1938 Evian Conference, accepted few refugees. Each step was tested against public reaction; too few objected. This gradual escalation is one of the Holocaust's most important lessons."],
    ["Ghettos and mass shootings", "Jews were forced into ghettos, and mobile killing units shot over a million people.",
      "After invading Poland in 1939, the Nazis forced Jews into overcrowded, sealed ghettos. In Warsaw's ghetto, over 400,000 people were crammed into a small area of the city, and tens of thousands died of starvation and disease. When Germany invaded the Soviet Union in June 1941, mobile killing units called Einsatzgruppen followed the army, helped by police battalions and local collaborators, shooting Jews in mass executions at pits outside towns and villages. At Babi Yar near Kyiv, over 33,000 Jews were murdered in two days in September 1941. Historians sometimes call this phase 'the Holocaust by bullets'."],
    ["The death camps", "The Nazis built extermination camps where millions were murdered in gas chambers.",
      "In January 1942, senior Nazi officials met at the Wannsee Conference to coordinate the 'Final Solution', the plan to murder all European Jews. Extermination camps, including Auschwitz-Birkenau, Treblinka, Sobibor, Belzec, Chelmno and Majdanek, were built in occupied Poland. Victims arrived in packed freight trains from across Europe, from France and the Netherlands to Greece. Most were murdered in gas chambers on arrival; others were selected for slave labour, where many were worked to death. About 1.1 million people were killed at Auschwitz alone, around a million of them Jews. At Treblinka, around 800,000 or more were murdered in little over a year."],
    ["Resistance and rescue", "Some Jews resisted, and some non-Jews risked their lives to rescue them.",
      "Despite overwhelming odds, Jews resisted: the Warsaw Ghetto Uprising of April 1943 held out for about a month against German troops, and prisoners revolted at Treblinka and Sobibor that year. Jewish partisans fought in forests across eastern Europe. Many resisted by preserving culture, education and records, like the hidden Oneg Shabbat archive, buried in milk cans and boxes in Warsaw and partly recovered after the war. Some non-Jews hid or rescued Jews at great risk; in 1943 Danes ferried most of Denmark's Jews to safety in Sweden. Israel's Yad Vashem memorial honours rescuers as Righteous Among the Nations. Most people, though, remained bystanders."],
    ["Liberation and aftermath", "Allied troops liberated the camps in 1944–45, but survivors faced loss, displacement and often nowhere to return.",
      "As Allied armies advanced, the SS forced prisoners on brutal 'death marches' away from the front, and many thousands died. Soviet troops reached Auschwitz on 27 January 1945, now marked as International Holocaust Remembrance Day. British soldiers entering Bergen-Belsen in April 1945 found thousands of unburied bodies; Anne Frank had died there of typhus only weeks earlier. Liberation did not end the suffering. Survivors were often sick and starving, their families murdered and their homes taken. Hundreds of thousands lived for years in displaced persons camps before emigrating to Israel, the United States, Australia and elsewhere."],
    ["Justice and new laws", "The Nuremberg trials and the new crime of genocide aimed to make such atrocities punishable under international law.",
      "From 1945 to 1946, surviving Nazi leaders were tried before an international military tribunal at Nuremberg for crimes including crimes against humanity; twelve were sentenced to death. Further trials followed for doctors, judges, industrialists and camp staff, though many perpetrators were never punished. In 1961 Israel tried Adolf Eichmann, a key organiser of the deportations, in a televised trial that brought survivors' testimony to the world. The word genocide was coined in 1944 by Polish-Jewish lawyer Raphael Lemkin, who had lost much of his family. The United Nations adopted the Genocide Convention in 1948, making genocide a crime under international law."],
    ["Why we remember", "The Holocaust shaped international law and remains a warning about hatred and indifference.",
      "Survivors, including many who rebuilt their lives in Australia, told their stories. Melbourne became home to one of the largest communities of Holocaust survivors per head of population outside Israel, and the Jewish Holocaust Centre there was founded by survivors in 1984. Holocaust denial persists, despite overwhelming evidence, including the Nazis' own records, photographs and thousands of testimonies. Remembering matters because the Holocaust was carried out by a modern, educated society, step by step, with laws, bureaucracy and ordinary officials, while many looked away. Its lessons point to noticing early warning signs: dehumanising language, scapegoating and the quiet removal of rights."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "About how many Jews were murdered in the Holocaust?", ["600,000", "Six million", "60 million", "One million"], 1, "About two-thirds of Europe's Jews."],
    [1, "What did the 1935 Nuremberg Laws do?", ["Freed prisoners", "Stripped Jews of citizenship", "Ended the war", "Created ghettos"], 1, "Persecution escalated step by step."],
    [3, "What was the Wannsee Conference?", ["A peace conference", "A 1942 meeting to coordinate the murder of European Jews", "A trial", "A resistance meeting"], 1, "The 'Final Solution'."],
    [4, "Whom does Yad Vashem honour as 'Righteous Among the Nations'?", ["Soldiers", "Non-Jews who risked their lives to rescue Jews", "Survivors", "Judges"], 1, "Most people remained bystanders."],
    [6, "Who coined the word 'genocide'?", ["Winston Churchill", "Raphael Lemkin", "Anne Frank", "Albert Einstein"], 1, "In 1944."],
  ]);

WB.L("end-of-empires", "history", "intermediate", "The end of empires",
  "In 1945, European empires ruled a large share of the world's people. Within about three decades, most colonies had become independent nations.",
  [
    ["The world in 1945", "After WWII, European empires still controlled large parts of Africa, Asia and the Pacific.",
      "In the early 20th century, Britain's empire covered about a quarter of the world's land, and after World War I it grew larger still. France, the Netherlands, Belgium and Portugal also held vast colonies. World War II weakened the European powers economically and militarily, and damaged their prestige: Japan had defeated European forces across Asia, capturing Singapore from Britain in 1942. Colonised peoples who had fought for the Allies asked why freedom should apply to Europe but not to them. The 1941 Atlantic Charter had promised people the right to choose their own government, and the new United States and Soviet superpowers both opposed old-style empires."],
    ["India's independence", "India won independence in 1947, but partition caused mass violence and displacement.",
      "India's independence movement, led by figures like Mahatma Gandhi and Jawaharlal Nehru through the Indian National Congress, used mass nonviolent protest against British rule, such as Gandhi's 1930 Salt March to the sea. After the war, a weakened Britain decided to leave quickly. In August 1947 it partitioned the subcontinent into mostly Hindu India and mostly Muslim Pakistan, which then included today's Bangladesh. The borders were drawn hastily. Partition triggered one of history's largest migrations: around 15 million people moved, and between hundreds of thousands and two million died in violence. Gandhi was assassinated in January 1948."],
    ["Wars of independence", "Some colonies won freedom only after bloody wars.",
      "Where colonial powers resisted, wars followed. Indonesia declared independence in 1945 and fought the Netherlands until 1949; Australian waterside workers supported it by refusing to load Dutch ships. Vietnam defeated France at Dien Bien Phu in 1954, before a long war involving the United States and Australia. Algeria's war against France (1954–1962) killed hundreds of thousands and brought down a French government. In Kenya, Britain brutally suppressed the Mau Mau uprising in the 1950s, detaining tens of thousands in camps. Portugal held on to Angola, Mozambique and Guinea-Bissau until a 1974 revolution in Lisbon, leaving them all by 1975."],
    ["The year of Africa", "In 1960 alone, 17 African countries gained independence.",
      "Ghana, led by Kwame Nkrumah, became the first sub-Saharan African colony to gain independence from Britain in 1957. In 1960, often called the Year of Africa, 17 nations became independent, including Nigeria, Somalia, the Congo and most of France's colonies. That year British Prime Minister Harold Macmillan told South Africa's parliament that a 'wind of change' was blowing through the continent. Belgium's hasty exit from the Congo was followed by years of crisis. By the late 1970s, most of Africa was independent, though white-minority rule lasted in Zimbabwe until 1980 and apartheid South Africa until 1994."],
    ["Challenges of independence", "New nations inherited artificial borders, weak institutions and economies built for export.",
      "Many colonial borders, drawn by Europeans at meetings such as the 1884–85 Berlin Conference, grouped rival groups together or split peoples apart. Colonial economies were often designed to export raw materials like cotton, cocoa, copper or rubber rather than develop local industry. Some colonies reached independence with very few university graduates to run a government. Some new nations built lasting democracies, like India and Botswana; others fell into civil wars, military coups or dictatorships, sometimes backed by Cold War superpowers. Historians continue to debate how much later problems stemmed from colonial legacies versus post-independence choices."],
    ["Choosing sides, or neither", "Many new nations tried to stay out of the Cold War through the Non-Aligned Movement.",
      "Independence arrived as the United States and the Soviet Union competed for influence, and both courted new nations with aid and weapons. In 1955, leaders from 29 Asian and African countries met at Bandung in Indonesia to promote cooperation and oppose colonialism. In 1961 the Non-Aligned Movement was founded in Belgrade, led by figures such as India's Nehru, Egypt's Gamal Abdel Nasser, Indonesia's Sukarno and Yugoslavia's Josip Broz Tito. The 1956 Suez Crisis, when Britain and France failed to retake the Suez Canal from Egypt under American pressure, showed how far the old imperial powers had declined."],
    ["Closer to home", "Papua New Guinea became independent from Australia in 1975.",
      "Australia administered Papua from 1906 and took over German New Guinea during World War I, later governing it under League of Nations and then United Nations mandates. The combined territory gained self-government in 1973 and became independent as Papua New Guinea on 16 September 1975, under its first prime minister, Michael Somare. Many Pacific nations gained independence in the same era, including Samoa (1962, from New Zealand), Nauru (1968, from Australian-led administration) and Fiji (1970, from Britain). Today the United Nations has 193 member states, up from 51 founding members in 1945."],
    ["Legacies today", "The end of empire still shapes languages, borders, migration and politics around the world.",
      "Imperial history is still visible everywhere. English, French, Spanish and Portuguese are spoken across continents because of it. The Commonwealth of Nations, a voluntary association of mostly former British territories, has over 50 members, including Australia. Britain returned Hong Kong to China in 1997, and Timor-Leste, abandoned by Portugal in 1975 and then occupied by Indonesia, became independent in 2002. The UN still lists around 17 'non-self-governing territories'. New Caledonia, a French territory near Australia, voted against independence in referendums in 2018, 2020 and 2021. Debates continue about apologies, reparations and returning museum artefacts."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did WWII affect European empires?", ["Strengthened them", "Weakened them economically, militarily and in prestige", "Had no effect", "Created new ones in Africa"], 1, "Japan's victories damaged European prestige."],
    [1, "What happened at India's independence in 1947?", ["It stayed British", "It was partitioned into India and Pakistan", "It joined China", "It became a monarchy"], 1, "Around 15 million people moved."],
    [2, "Where did France fight a long war of independence from 1954 to 1962?", ["India", "Algeria", "Kenya", "Indonesia"], 1, "Hundreds of thousands were killed."],
    [3, "Why is 1960 called the Year of Africa?", ["A famous summit", "17 African countries gained independence", "A World Cup", "A famine ended"], 1, "Macmillan spoke of a 'wind of change'."],
    [6, "When did Papua New Guinea gain independence from Australia?", ["1901", "1945", "1975", "1999"], 2, "16 September 1975."],
  ]);

WB.L("civil-rights", "history", "intermediate", "The civil rights movement",
  "Bus boycotts, sit-ins and marches. How ordinary people used nonviolent protest to dismantle legal segregation in America, and inspired Australians too.",
  [
    ["Jim Crow", "After slavery ended, southern US states imposed segregation laws known as Jim Crow.",
      "After the Civil War ended slavery in 1865, southern states passed laws segregating Black and white Americans in schools, transport, restaurants, hospitals and even cemeteries, and used poll taxes, literacy tests and intimidation to stop Black citizens voting. In Plessy v. Ferguson (1896), the Supreme Court approved 'separate but equal' facilities, though in practice Black facilities were far worse. Lynchings, public murders by white mobs, terrorised Black communities; thousands were killed. Millions of Black southerners moved to northern and western cities in the Great Migration, but met discrimination there too. This system of laws and customs was known as Jim Crow."],
    ["Brown v. Board", "In 1954 the Supreme Court ruled segregated public schools unconstitutional.",
      "Lawyers from the NAACP, the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People, led by Thurgood Marshall, spent years building legal challenges to school segregation. In Brown v. Board of Education (1954), the Supreme Court unanimously ruled that separate schools were inherently unequal, overturning the reasoning of Plessy for public education. Many southern states resisted fiercely, a campaign politicians called 'massive resistance', and some closed schools rather than integrate. In 1957, nine Black students, the Little Rock Nine, needed federal troops to escort them into a high school in Arkansas. Marshall later became the first Black Supreme Court justice, in 1967."],
    ["The Montgomery bus boycott", "Rosa Parks's arrest in 1955 sparked a year-long bus boycott led by Martin Luther King Jr.",
      "In December 1955, Rosa Parks, an experienced activist and secretary of the local NAACP branch, refused to give up her bus seat to a white passenger in Montgomery, Alabama, and was arrested. Black residents, who made up most of the bus company's riders, boycotted the city's buses for 381 days, walking long distances or sharing organised carpools. A 26-year-old minister, Martin Luther King Jr., emerged as a leader, and his house was bombed. The boycott ended in December 1956 after the Supreme Court ruled bus segregation unconstitutional. It showed the power of organised, disciplined, nonviolent mass action."],
    ["Sit-ins and marches", "Nonviolent protests drew national attention, culminating in the 1963 March on Washington.",
      "In February 1960, four Black students sat at a whites-only lunch counter in Greensboro, North Carolina, and refused to leave; sit-ins spread across the South. In 1961, Freedom Riders rode buses through the South to challenge segregated terminals and were violently attacked, one bus firebombed. In 1963, police in Birmingham, Alabama, turned fire hoses and dogs on young protesters, shocking the nation on television. That August, around 250,000 people joined the March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom, where King gave his 'I Have a Dream' speech. Television coverage helped turn local struggles into a national issue."],
    ["Laws that changed the nation", "The Civil Rights Act of 1964 and Voting Rights Act of 1965 outlawed segregation and discrimination in voting.",
      "After President John F. Kennedy's assassination in November 1963, President Lyndon B. Johnson pushed Congress to pass the Civil Rights Act of 1964, banning segregation in public places and discrimination in employment. In March 1965, state troopers attacked peaceful marchers on the Edmund Pettus Bridge in Selma, Alabama, on 'Bloody Sunday'. Months later the Voting Rights Act outlawed literacy tests and other discriminatory voting practices and sent federal officials to register voters. Black voter registration in the South rose dramatically; in Mississippi it jumped from under 10% to around 60% within a few years. The legal foundations of Jim Crow were gone."],
    ["Beyond nonviolence", "By the late 1960s, some activists rejected nonviolence, and King's assassination in 1968 shook the movement.",
      "Not everyone shared King's strategy. Malcolm X, a powerful speaker for the Nation of Islam, argued Black Americans had a right to self-defence and criticised integration as a goal; he was assassinated in 1965. From 1966, the slogan 'Black Power' called for pride, self-reliance and political power, and the Black Panther Party organised armed patrols and community breakfast programs. Riots erupted in many cities, often triggered by police incidents. King broadened his focus to poverty and the Vietnam War before he was assassinated in Memphis on 4 April 1968. A week later, Congress passed the Fair Housing Act."],
    ["The Australian Freedom Ride", "In 1965, Charles Perkins led a Freedom Ride through NSW to expose discrimination against Aboriginal people.",
      "Inspired by the American movement, University of Sydney students led by Charles Perkins, an Arrernte and Kalkadoon man who in 1966 became one of the first Aboriginal university graduates, toured country towns in NSW by bus in February 1965. They surveyed living conditions and exposed segregation at swimming pools, clubs, cinemas and shops. In Moree, where Aboriginal children were barred from the town pool, and in Walgett, the students faced angry crowds. The publicity helped build support for the 1967 referendum, in which about 90.8% of Australians voted to count Aboriginal people in the census and let the federal government make laws for them."],
    ["Unfinished business", "The movement ended legal segregation, but debates about equality, voting and policing continue.",
      "The civil rights movement transformed American law and politics. Black elected officials, rare in the South in 1965, now number in the thousands, and in 2008 Barack Obama was elected the first Black president. Yet gaps in wealth, health, schooling and incarceration rates remain large, and arguments continue. In 2013 the Supreme Court struck down a key part of the Voting Rights Act, and later protests, such as those after George Floyd's murder by a police officer in 2020, echoed earlier struggles. The movement's methods, patient organising, boycotts, legal challenges and disciplined nonviolent protest, are still studied by campaigners worldwide."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What were Jim Crow laws?", ["Anti-slavery laws", "Laws enforcing segregation in the US South", "Farming laws", "Immigration laws"], 1, "Plus barriers to voting."],
    [1, "What did Brown v. Board of Education rule?", ["Segregated schools were fine", "Segregated public schools were unconstitutional", "Schools should close", "Only private schools could integrate"], 1, "Unanimously, in 1954."],
    [2, "What sparked the Montgomery bus boycott?", ["A bus crash", "Rosa Parks's arrest for refusing to give up her seat", "A fare increase", "A speech"], 1, "It lasted over a year."],
    [4, "What did the Voting Rights Act of 1965 do?", ["Created elections", "Outlawed discriminatory voting practices", "Lowered the voting age", "Banned political parties"], 1, "Passed after Selma."],
    [6, "Who led Australia's 1965 Freedom Ride?", ["Eddie Mabo", "Charles Perkins", "Neville Bonner", "Vincent Lingiari"], 1, "Exposing discrimination in NSW towns."],
  ]);

WB.L("first-fleet", "history", "intermediate", "The First Fleet and colonisation",
  "In 1788, eleven ships arrived in Sydney Cove. For Britain it was a new colony; for the land's Aboriginal owners, it was an invasion.",
  [
    ["A continent already home", "Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples had lived in Australia for more than 50,000 years, possibly 65,000 or more.",
      "When Europeans arrived, Australia was home to hundreds of distinct Aboriginal nations and more than 250 languages, with Torres Strait Islander peoples to the north. Archaeologists agree people have lived here for more than 50,000 years, and excavations at Madjedbebe in Arnhem Land suggest about 65,000, though that date is still debated. Aboriginal peoples managed the land with fire, fish traps and trade networks, and lived by complex systems of law, kinship and knowledge. Dutch sailors had mapped parts of the coast from 1606, and James Cook charted the east coast in 1770, claiming it for Britain despite its people."],
    ["Why Britain came", "Britain sent convicts to Australia after losing its American colonies.",
      "Britain had shipped around 50,000 convicts to its American colonies until the American Revolution ended that option in the 1770s. Harsh laws made many minor thefts serious crimes, and prisons and old ships used as floating prisons, called hulks, overflowed. In 1786 the government chose Botany Bay, which Cook's expedition had visited and botanist Joseph Banks had recommended, as a new penal colony. Strategic interests, like a base in the Pacific and access to timber and flax for the navy, may also have played a role; historians still debate how much. Captain Arthur Phillip, a naval officer, was appointed commander and first governor."],
    ["The voyage", "The First Fleet of 11 ships carried around 1,400 people, about half of them convicts.",
      "The First Fleet left Portsmouth on 13 May 1787 and sailed for over eight months, via Tenerife, Rio de Janeiro and Cape Town, where it loaded livestock and seeds. Its eleven ships, two navy vessels, six convict transports and three store ships, carried roughly 1,400 people, including about 750 convicts, marines, officers, sailors and some families. Most convicts had been sentenced for theft; the oldest was in her eighties and the youngest was a child. Botany Bay proved unsuitable, with poor soil and little fresh water, so Phillip moved to Port Jackson, and on 26 January 1788 raised the British flag at Sydney Cove."],
    ["Hard early years", "The colony nearly starved in its first years.",
      "Few convicts had farming skills. Crops failed in poor, sandy soils, cattle strayed into the bush, and supply ships were delayed; the store ship Guardian was wrecked in 1789. Rations were cut severely, and Phillip sent people to Norfolk Island to ease the pressure. The Second Fleet arrived in 1790 with hundreds of convicts dead or dying from neglect by the private contractors who carried them. A smallpox epidemic in 1789 devastated Aboriginal communities around Sydney, killing a large share of the local population; how it began is still debated by historians. Only in the mid-1790s did farms along the Hawkesbury River ease food shortages."],
    ["First encounters", "Early relations with the Eora people mixed curiosity, kidnapping, negotiation and violence.",
      "Relations with the Eora and neighbouring peoples began with some curiosity but quickly deteriorated as colonists took fish, land and water. Hoping to learn the local language and customs, Phillip had Aboriginal men captured. Arabanoo, seized at Manly in late 1788, died of smallpox in 1789. Bennelong, a Wangal man, was captured with Colebee later that year, escaped, then chose to return. In 1790 Phillip was speared in the shoulder at Manly Cove, but did not retaliate. Bennelong travelled to England with Phillip in 1792 and returned in 1795. His wife Barangaroo is now remembered in the name of a Sydney precinct."],
    ["Frontier conflict", "As settlement spread, frontier wars and dispossession devastated Aboriginal communities.",
      "Britain treated Australia as terra nullius in practice, land belonging to no one, and made no treaties, apart from John Batman's unrecognised 1835 attempt near Melbourne. As colonists took land for farming and grazing, Aboriginal people resisted, and violent frontier conflicts continued for over a century, into the 1920s. Warriors like Pemulwuy, a Bidjigal man, led resistance around Sydney until he was killed in 1802. Massacres of Aboriginal people occurred across the country; after the Myall Creek massacre in 1838, seven colonists were hanged, a rare case of punishment. Disease, violence and loss of land caused catastrophic population decline."],
    ["A convict colony grows", "Transportation continued until 1868, and many convicts built new lives as free settlers.",
      "Between 1788 and 1868, about 162,000 convicts were transported to the Australian colonies, to New South Wales, Van Diemen's Land (now Tasmania), Moreton Bay and, last of all, Western Australia. Well-behaved convicts could earn a 'ticket of leave' to work for themselves, and many who finished their sentences, known as emancipists, became farmers, merchants and builders. Governor Lachlan Macquarie (1810–1821) championed them, appointing emancipists to official posts and commissioning convict architect Francis Greenway. Free settlers arrived in growing numbers from the 1820s, and opposition to transportation grew. Many Australians today have convict ancestors, once a source of shame, now often of pride."],
    ["Remembering 26 January", "26 January is Australia Day, but many Aboriginal people mark it as Invasion Day or Survival Day.",
      "The anniversary of the First Fleet's landing gradually became a national holiday, observed on 26 January by all states and territories only from 1994. For many Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, it marks the beginning of dispossession. On 26 January 1938, the 150th anniversary, Aboriginal activists including William Cooper, Jack Patten and William Ferguson held a Day of Mourning in Sydney, and the date has been a day of protest ever since. Today Australians hold a range of views: some celebrate, some mourn, some want the date changed. Understanding these perspectives is part of understanding Australian history."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "When did Dutch sailors begin mapping parts of Australia's coast?", ["1606", "1770", "1788", "1851"], 0, "Dutch ships charted parts of the coast from 1606, more than 160 years before James Cook reached the east coast."],
    [1, "Why did Britain start a penal colony in Australia?", ["For gold", "It could no longer send convicts to America", "To trade with China", "For tourism"], 1, "Prisons were overflowing."],
    [2, "Where did Phillip establish the colony on 26 January 1788?", ["Botany Bay", "Sydney Cove at Port Jackson", "Hobart", "Melbourne"], 1, "Botany Bay proved unsuitable."],
    [5, "What was the Myall Creek massacre?", ["A battle with France", "A massacre of Aboriginal people in 1838", "A convict revolt", "A shipwreck"], 1, "Frontier violence spread across the country."],
    [7, "What did Aboriginal activists hold on 26 January 1938?", ["A celebration", "A Day of Mourning", "A sports carnival", "An election"], 1, "The date remains contested."],
  ]);

WB.L("gold-rush", "history", "beginner", "The gold rushes",
  "In the 1850s, gold transformed Australia almost overnight, nearly tripling its population and sparking its most famous rebellion.",
  [
    ["Gold!", "In 1851 gold was discovered in NSW and then Victoria, triggering massive gold rushes.",
      "In February 1851, Edward Hargraves, freshly back from the California gold rush, found gold near Bathurst in New South Wales and publicised it widely. Within months, even richer finds were made in the new colony of Victoria, at Ballarat, Bendigo and Mount Alexander near Castlemaine. Victoria became one of the richest goldfields in the world, producing a large share of the world's gold during the 1850s. Shepherds, clerks, sailors, shopkeepers and servants abandoned their jobs to head to the diggings. Some ships in Melbourne's harbour were left stranded when their crews deserted for the goldfields."],
    ["A population boom", "Australia's non-Indigenous population nearly tripled in the 1850s.",
      "News of gold spread around the world, and people poured in from Britain, Ireland, Europe, the United States and China. Australia's non-Indigenous population grew from about 430,000 in 1851 to over 1.1 million by 1861, and Victoria's grew roughly sevenfold. Many newcomers were young, educated and skilled, and brought ideas about democracy with them. Gold wealth paid for grand public buildings, railways and banks, and Melbourne grew rapidly into one of the world's richest cities, earning the nickname 'Marvellous Melbourne' by the 1880s. The rushes changed Australia from a collection of convict and pastoral colonies into a diverse, wealthy society."],
    ["Life on the diggings", "Life on the goldfields was hard, crowded and unpredictable.",
      "Diggers lived in tents and bark huts, working claims with picks, shovels, pans and wooden cradles to wash gold from dirt. As surface gold ran out, they sank deep shafts, and later companies used machinery to crush quartz rock. Water, food and supplies were expensive, and diseases like dysentery spread in crowded camps. A few struck it rich, finding huge nuggets like the 'Welcome Stranger', found near Moliagul in Victoria in 1869 and weighing around 72 kilograms. Most found little. Many made their fortunes not from digging but by selling supplies, food, transport and services to the diggers."],
    ["The Chinese diggers", "Tens of thousands of Chinese miners came, facing heavy discrimination.",
      "By the late 1850s, around 40,000 Chinese people were in Victoria, mostly men from southern China seeking gold. They often worked in organised groups, patiently reworking claims others had abandoned. Resentment grew, leading to violent attacks, like the Buckland River riot in Victoria in 1857 and the Lambing Flat riots in NSW in 1860–61. Colonies imposed special taxes on Chinese arrivals, so many ships landed at Robe in South Australia instead, and passengers walked hundreds of kilometres to the Victorian fields. This hostility fed into the later White Australia policy. Chinese communities also left lasting legacies, from market gardens to Chinatowns."],
    ["Eureka Stockade", "In 1854, miners at Ballarat rebelled against licence fees and harsh policing at the Eureka Stockade.",
      "Miners had to pay for an expensive monthly licence to dig, 30 shillings at first, whether they found gold or not, and police conducted brutal 'licence hunts'. Diggers had no vote. Anger boiled over at Ballarat in 1854 after a hotel keeper was acquitted of killing a miner, and the hotel was burned. Miners formed the Ballarat Reform League, then, led by Peter Lalor, swore allegiance to the blue-and-white Southern Cross flag and built a rough stockade. Early on 3 December 1854, soldiers and police attacked; the fight lasted about 15 minutes, and around 30 people died, mostly miners."],
    ["Eureka's legacy", "Eureka helped win voting rights and became a symbol of Australian democracy.",
      "Public sympathy swung behind the miners. In 1855, Melbourne juries refused to convict 13 men charged with treason. The licence was replaced with a cheaper miner's right costing one pound a year, which came with a vote. Within a couple of years, Victoria introduced voting reforms, including the secret ballot in 1856, where voters mark a government-printed paper in private, so influential it became known worldwide as the 'Australian ballot'. Peter Lalor, who lost an arm in the fight, later became a member of parliament. The Eureka flag remains a potent, and contested, political symbol."],
    ["Later rushes", "Gold rushes continued for decades, most famously in Queensland and Western Australia.",
      "Gold fever did not end with the 1850s. Finds at Gympie in 1867 helped rescue Queensland from debt, and the Palmer River rush in the 1870s drew thousands of Chinese miners to Cape York. In 1892 Arthur Bayley and William Ford found gold at Coolgardie in Western Australia, and in 1893 Paddy Hannan's find at Kalgoorlie started another stampede. Water was so scarce that engineer C. Y. O'Connor designed a pipeline of over 500 kilometres from near Perth, completed in 1903. The WA rush multiplied that colony's population, and many eastern miners there helped sway it towards joining Federation."],
    ["What gold left behind", "Gold built cities and democracy, but came at a heavy cost to Aboriginal peoples and the land.",
      "The rushes left grand streetscapes in towns like Ballarat, Bendigo and Beechworth, and Sovereign Hill in Ballarat now recreates goldfields life for visitors. They also sped up the dispossession of Aboriginal peoples, such as the Wadawurrung around Ballarat and the Dja Dja Wurrung around Bendigo, whose lands and waterways were overrun and damaged. Digging and sluicing stripped forests and choked creeks with silt. Gold remains important: Australia is one of the world's largest gold producers, with much of it mined around Kalgoorlie. When you visit an old goldfields town, look for the Chinese joss houses, mullock heaps and ornate banks."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where were Victoria's richest early goldfields?", ["Sydney and Newcastle", "Ballarat and Bendigo", "Perth and Kalgoorlie", "Hobart"], 1, "Victoria became one of the richest goldfields in the world."],
    [1, "How did Australia's population change in the 1850s?", ["It halved", "It nearly tripled", "It stayed the same", "It doubled every year"], 1, "From about 430,000 to over 1.1 million."],
    [2, "How did many people really profit from gold rushes?", ["Finding huge nuggets", "Selling supplies and services to diggers", "Gambling", "Government jobs"], 1, "Most diggers found little."],
    [4, "What caused the Eureka rebellion?", ["A flood", "Expensive licence fees, harsh policing and no vote", "Chinese immigration", "A bank collapse"], 1, "Led by Peter Lalor in 1854."],
    [5, "What reform became known worldwide as the 'Australian ballot'?", ["Compulsory voting", "The secret ballot", "Women's suffrage", "Postal voting"], 1, "Introduced in Victoria in 1856."],
  ]);

WB.L("federation", "society", "beginner", "Federation: how Australia became a nation",
  "Until 1901, 'Australia' was six separate British colonies with their own armies, stamps and tariffs. Then they voted to unite.",
  [
    ["Six colonies", "Before 1901, Australia was six self-governing British colonies.",
      "By the late 1800s, New South Wales, Victoria, Queensland, South Australia, Western Australia and Tasmania each had their own parliament, laws, defence forces and postage stamps. They even charged tariffs on goods crossing their borders, with customs houses along the Murray River, and used different railway gauges, so passengers and cargo had to change trains at the NSW–Victoria border at Albury. Victoria protected local industry with high tariffs, while NSW favoured free trade, a rivalry that shaped the whole debate. Britain still controlled foreign affairs. Many people argued the colonies would be stronger, richer and safer together."],
    ["Why unite?", "Defence, trade, immigration and a growing national identity drove federation.",
      "Supporters argued a united nation could defend itself better, especially as Germany, France and other powers expanded in the Pacific; an 1889 British military report recommended a federated defence force. Free trade between colonies would help business, and the severe depression of the early 1890s made cooperation more appealing. A national government could control immigration, a goal tragically tied to racist aims of keeping Australia 'white'. And by the 1890s, most colonists were Australian-born, and a sense of Australian identity was growing, expressed in art by painters like Tom Roberts and in the bush poetry of Henry Lawson and Banjo Paterson."],
    ["The father of Federation", "Sir Henry Parkes's 1889 Tenterfield Oration pushed federation forward.",
      "NSW Premier Henry Parkes, now called the father of Federation, gave a famous speech at Tenterfield in 1889 calling for a national government and a national army. He chaired an 1891 convention in Sydney that produced a draft constitution, but momentum stalled. Ordinary citizens revived it: at the 1893 Corowa conference, a proposal was made for the people to elect delegates to a new convention. Elected delegates met in 1897 and 1898 in Adelaide, Sydney and Melbourne. Edmund Barton, who would become the first prime minister, and Alfred Deakin, a Victorian lawyer, were key figures. The drafters borrowed from both British and American systems."],
    ["The people vote", "Australians voted to approve the Constitution in referendums between 1898 and 1900.",
      "Unusually for the time, the Constitution was approved by voters in referendums held in each colony, rather than simply imposed. A first round in 1898 fell short in NSW, which had set a minimum number of yes votes, and after some changes a second round passed in 1899. Western Australia joined last, in 1900, after the promise of a transcontinental railway and pressure from goldfields residents. Only some people could vote: in most colonies, women and many Aboriginal people were excluded. The British Parliament then passed the Constitution as an Act, and Queen Victoria gave royal assent in July 1900."],
    ["1 January 1901", "The Commonwealth of Australia was proclaimed in Sydney on 1 January 1901.",
      "The new nation was proclaimed in Centennial Park, Sydney, before a crowd of perhaps 60,000 or more, and the first Governor-General, Lord Hopetoun, swore in the ministry. Edmund Barton became prime minister. The first federal parliament met in Melbourne in May 1901, which served as the temporary capital until Canberra, a purpose-built compromise between rivals Sydney and Melbourne, designed by American architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, was ready in 1927. One of the new parliament's first laws was the Immigration Restriction Act, the foundation of the White Australia policy."],
    ["What Federation created", "Federation created a federal system dividing power between the Commonwealth and the states.",
      "The Constitution divided powers: the federal government handles areas like defence, foreign affairs, trade, immigration and currency, mostly listed in section 51; the states keep powers over areas like schools, hospitals, police and roads, though federal funding now shapes many of these. A High Court interprets the Constitution and settles disputes between governments. Parliament has two houses: the House of Representatives, where government is formed, and the Senate, where each original state has equal representation regardless of population. Changes require a referendum approved by a majority of voters nationally and in a majority of states, which is why only 8 of 45 proposals had passed by 2023."],
    ["Who had a voice", "Early federal democracy gave most women the vote in 1902 but excluded most Aboriginal people.",
      "In some ways the new nation was a democratic pioneer. South Australia had given women the vote and the right to stand for parliament in 1894, a world first for standing. The Commonwealth Franchise Act of 1902 gave most women the right to vote and stand in federal elections, making Australia one of the first countries to do so. But the same Act excluded most Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and all Aboriginal Australians did not gain the federal vote until 1962. New Zealand took part in early federation talks and is even named in the Constitution's opening clauses, but chose not to join."],
    ["Federation in your life", "Knowing which level of government does what helps you get things done.",
      "Federation still shapes daily life. Australians vote in federal, state or territory and local elections, and local councils, though important, are not mentioned in the Constitution; referendums to recognise them failed in 1974 and 1988. The ACT and Northern Territory are territories, not states, so the federal parliament can override their laws, and each elects two senators, while each state elects twelve. When you have a problem, it helps to know who is responsible: Centrelink, passports and Medicare are federal; public hospitals, schools, driver licences and police are state matters; bins, local roads and building permits usually belong to councils."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What problem did different railway gauges cause?", ["Faster trains", "Passengers and cargo had to change trains at borders", "Cheaper tickets", "Nothing"], 1, "Each colony built its own."],
    [2, "Who is called the father of Federation?", ["Edmund Barton", "Henry Parkes", "Alfred Deakin", "Banjo Paterson"], 1, "His Tenterfield Oration was in 1889."],
    [3, "How was the Constitution approved?", ["By the Queen alone", "By referendums in the colonies", "By the army", "By the governors"], 1, "Unusual for the time."],
    [4, "Where was Australia's first federal parliament held?", ["Canberra", "Sydney", "Melbourne", "Adelaide"], 2, "Canberra was ready in 1927."],
    [5, "How many of 45 referendum proposals have passed?", ["8", "20", "30", "45"], 0, "A double majority is required."],
  ]);

WB.L("modern-australia", "history", "intermediate", "Post-war Australia: migration and change",
  "'Populate or perish.' After 1945, millions of migrants, the end of White Australia and new recognition of First Nations rights reshaped the country.",
  [
    ["Populate or perish", "After WWII, Australia launched a huge immigration program.",
      "World War II, especially the Japanese bombing of Darwin in 1942 and the fear of invasion, convinced leaders Australia needed a bigger population for defence and industry. Immigration Minister Arthur Calwell's slogan was 'populate or perish', and the Department of Immigration was created in 1945. Australia first accepted about 170,000 displaced people from war-torn Europe, then large numbers of British, Italian, Greek, Dutch, German, Maltese and other migrants. Many British migrants paid just ten pounds for the voyage, earning the nickname 'Ten Pound Poms'. Between 1945 and the mid-1970s, roughly three million migrants arrived, many working on nation-building projects."],
    ["The Snowy Mountains Scheme", "Built from 1949 to 1974, it became a symbol of multicultural, nation-building Australia.",
      "The Snowy Mountains Hydro-Electric Scheme was a vast engineering project of 16 major dams, 7 power stations and around 145 kilometres of tunnels, built between 1949 and 1974. It diverts water from the Snowy River westward through the mountains, generating electricity and supplying irrigation water to the Murray and Murrumbidgee river systems. Over 100,000 people from more than 30 countries worked on it, many recent migrants who had been on opposite sides in the war. More than 100 workers died during construction. It's often seen as a symbol of post-war Australia's ambition and emerging multicultural society, and is still a major source of renewable electricity."],
    ["Ending White Australia", "The White Australia policy was dismantled in stages, ending formally in 1973.",
      "Since 1901, the White Australia policy had restricted non-European immigration, often using a dictation test of 50 words in any European language chosen by officials, designed to be failed. It was gradually relaxed from the 1950s and 60s: the Colombo Plan brought thousands of Asian students to Australian universities, the dictation test was abolished in 1958, and the Holt government eased restrictions in 1966. In 1973, the Whitlam government removed race as a factor in immigration selection. After the Vietnam War, Australia accepted tens of thousands of Vietnamese refugees in the late 1970s and 80s under the Fraser government, a major step towards a multicultural society."],
    ["The 1967 referendum", "In 1967, over 90% of voters agreed to count Aboriginal people in the census and allow federal laws for them.",
      "The 1967 referendum removed two references in the Constitution that discriminated against Aboriginal people: one excluded them from population counts, and the other barred the Commonwealth from making laws for them. After a decade-long campaign by Aboriginal and non-Aboriginal activists, about 90.8% of voters said yes, the highest ever for a referendum. It's often misunderstood: it didn't give Aboriginal people citizenship or the vote, which they already had in law federally from 1962. But it was a powerful symbol of national support and gave the Commonwealth power to make laws for Aboriginal people, which later underpinned land rights and heritage laws."],
    ["Land rights and Mabo", "The Mabo decision in 1992 overturned terra nullius and recognised native title.",
      "Aboriginal activism grew: the 1966 Wave Hill walk-off by Gurindji stockmen led by Vincent Lingiari began as a protest over pay and conditions and became a land rights struggle. In 1975 Prime Minister Gough Whitlam poured soil into Lingiari's hand, symbolically returning land. The Aboriginal Tent Embassy was set up in Canberra in 1972. Eddie Mabo, a Meriam man from Mer (Murray Island) in the Torres Strait, challenged Queensland in court. In 1992, months after Mabo's death, the High Court's Mabo decision rejected terra nullius and recognised that native title could survive, leading to the Native Title Act 1993."],
    ["Reckoning and debate", "Recent decades have brought apologies, reconciliation efforts and ongoing debate.",
      "The 1997 Bringing Them Home report documented the Stolen Generations, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children forcibly removed from their families by governments and churches, estimating between one in three and one in ten children were taken between 1910 and 1970. In 2000, around 250,000 people walked across Sydney Harbour Bridge for reconciliation. In February 2008, Prime Minister Kevin Rudd delivered a formal National Apology in parliament. The 2017 Uluru Statement from the Heart called for a First Nations Voice. In 2023, a referendum proposing an Indigenous Voice to Parliament was defeated, with about 60% voting no. Debate about treaties and truth-telling continues."],
    ["New waves of migration", "Since the 1970s, migrants from Asia and beyond have made Australia one of the world's most multicultural nations.",
      "After White Australia ended, migration became far more diverse. Refugees came from Vietnam, Cambodia and Lebanon, later from the former Yugoslavia, Africa and the Middle East, and skilled migrants and students from China, India, the Philippines and elsewhere. Governments adopted multiculturalism as official policy, and SBS was created to broadcast in many languages, with SBS television starting in 1980. Today around 30% of Australia's population was born overseas, and roughly half of Australians have at least one parent born overseas. After England, India, China and New Zealand are among the most common countries of birth, and over 300 languages are spoken at home."],
    ["A changing society", "Post-war Australia also transformed its economy, health system, family law and place in the world.",
      "Migration was only part of the change. Women won equal pay decisions in 1969 and 1972, and the Family Law Act 1975 introduced no-fault divorce. Universal health insurance began as Medibank in 1975 and returned as Medicare in 1984. The Sydney Opera House opened in 1973, becoming an international symbol of the country. From 1983 the Hawke and Keating governments floated the dollar and cut tariffs, opening the economy to the world, while trade shifted from Britain towards Japan, China and the rest of Asia. Many of the institutions Australians take for granted today date from these post-war decades."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was Arthur Calwell's slogan?", ["Advance Australia Fair", "Populate or perish", "Fair go for all", "Australia first"], 1, "Driving post-war immigration."],
    [1, "What was the Snowy Mountains Scheme?", ["A ski resort", "A hydro-electric scheme built largely by migrants", "A national park", "A railway"], 1, "Over 100,000 workers from 30+ countries."],
    [2, "When was race formally removed from immigration selection?", ["1901", "1945", "1973", "2000"], 2, "Under the Whitlam government."],
    [3, "What did the 1967 referendum NOT do?", ["Count Aboriginal people in the census", "Give Aboriginal people citizenship or the vote", "Let the federal government make laws for them", "Pass with over 90%"], 1, "A common misunderstanding."],
    [4, "Who led the 1966 Wave Hill walk-off by Gurindji stockmen?", ["Eddie Mabo", "Charles Perkins", "Vincent Lingiari", "Neville Bonner"], 2, "It began as a protest over pay and conditions and became a landmark land rights struggle."],
  ]);
